Filariasis is caused by filarial worms. Two such helminths are:
- **Wuchereria bancrofti**;
- **Wuchereria malayi**.

Chronic manifestations (the worms live in the lymphatic vessels for many years):
- slowly developing **chronic inflammation of the organs** in which the worms live, for many years;
- **inflammation of the lymphatic vessels of the lower limbs**, giving gross swelling of the legs (elephantiasis);
- **deformities of the genital organs**.

Any two of these are expected. The pathogens are transmitted to a healthy person through the bite of the female mosquito vectors.

Smoking is harmful in many ways. Four important hazards (any four):
- **increased carbon monoxide (CO) content in the blood**, which reduces the concentration of haem-bound oxygen and causes **oxygen deficiency** in the body;
- increased incidence of **cancers** of the lung, urinary bladder and throat;
- **bronchitis** and **emphysema**;
- **coronary heart disease**;
- **gastric ulcer**;
- **raised blood pressure** and **increased heart rate**.

(i) When prickly pear cactus was introduced into Australia, it **did not have its natural predators** there (the herbivores that feed on it in its native habitat). With nothing to keep its population in check, it multiplied unchecked and spread rapidly over millions of hectares of rangeland. The invasion was brought under control only after a cactus-feeding predator (a moth) from its natural habitat was introduced into the country.

(ii) Importance of the prey-predator relationship in a habitat (any two):
- predators act as **conduits for energy transfer** across trophic levels;
- they **keep prey populations under control**, which would otherwise reach very high densities and cause ecosystem instability;
- they help in **maintaining species diversity** in a community by reducing the intensity of competition among competing prey species;
- predators are **prudent**: if a predator were too efficient and overexploited its prey, the prey would become extinct and the predator, lacking food, would also become extinct.

(i) The introduction of the Nile perch, a predatory fish, into Lake Victoria in East Africa led eventually to the **extinction of an ecologically unique assemblage of more than 200 species of cichlid fish** in the lake. This is an example of loss of biodiversity caused by an alien species invasion.

(ii) Invasive weed species that pose a threat to our native species (any two):
- **carrot grass (Parthenium)**;
- **Lantana**;
- **water hyacinth (Eichhornia)**.

The cloning vector pBR322 is drawn as a circular plasmid carrying the **ampicillin resistance gene (ampR)** and the **tetracycline resistance gene (tetR)**, with these labels:
- (I) a restriction site in the tetracycline resistance gene: **BamH I** or **Sal I**;
- (II) a restriction site in the ampicillin resistance gene: **Pvu I** or **Pst I**;
- (III) the **ori** site, the origin of replication.

Other features that may be shown are the rop region and the sites of EcoR I, Cla I, Hind III and Pvu II. The marking scheme gives a half mark each for any two correct labellings.
